{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git show 99037c9'","aggregated_output":"commit 99037c9a30625d9b1b6500400c4f45ab7294a519\nAuthor: Stuart Bowness <1020179+stuartbowness@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Fri May 1 06:44:01 2026 -0700\n\n    [task-3] Rename contract assertions to checks in prompts, templates, docs, and README\n\ndiff --git a/README.md b/README.md\nindex 822cbcf..94afaba 100644\n--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -81,7 +81,7 @@ Every tasks.md ends with a set of **mandatory closing tasks** that run after the\n \n After the tasks are defined, the orchestrator runs a **coverage audit**. This maps every requirement from the PRD to one or more tasks. If anything is missing, it flags it. If a requirement was intentionally changed or dropped, you approve that decision explicitly. For substitutions, the orchestrator also checks that the replacement approach is fully covered by existing tasks -- if the substitution drops something important, it's flagged as a gap. Nothing falls through the cracks by accident.\n \n-Contract assertions trace back to specific spec capability and scenario identifiers for end-to-end traceability.\n+Contract checks trace back to specific spec capability and scenario identifiers for end-to-end traceability.\n \n Finally, it writes a **contract** -- a checklist of things the finished code should do, written so they can be tested from the outside (by running the code, not by reading the source). For example: \"the login endpoint returns a 401 when given wrong credentials.\" Each item traces back to a specific spec scenario and is tagged with how it should be verified (by executing the code, checking its output, or inspecting the source). Before moving on, the orchestrator runs a testability check to catch any vague or untestable items early. You review the contract, confirm which AI models handle each role (see [Configuration](#configuration)), and approve launch.\n \ndiff --git a/docs/architecture.png b/docs/architecture.png\nindex 08b8c8c..fa2c1f8 100644\nBinary files a/docs/architecture.png and b/docs/architecture.png differ\ndiff --git a/docs/design-decisions.md b/docs/design-decisions.md\nindex d4e44f3..70e3890 100644\n--- a/docs/design-decisions.md\n+++ b/docs/design-decisions.md\n@@ -34,14 +34,14 @@ Inner loop per task: `spec -> build -> verify -> ship` with repetition on valida\n - Default engine: Codex (configurable in mission/config.yaml)\n \n ### Per-task validators (run sequentially)\n-1. **Scrutiny validator** -- test presence gate, build/test/lint, /review, code standards, /simplify, /security (conditional), contract assertions. Always runs.\n+1. **Scrutiny validator** -- test presence gate, build/test/lint, /review, code standards, /simplify, /security (conditional), contract checks. Always runs.\n 2. **User-test validator** -- decides API tests (HTTP) and/or browser tests (agent-browser) based on what changed. Always runs.\n 3. **Demo video** -- recorded after both validators pass, for frontend tasks. Orchestrator decides when.\n \n ### End-of-mission review\n 4. **Dual adversarial review** -- runs once after the draft PR is created, against the full diff. Codex and Claude independently review the code. Both reports are presented to the human. No automated fix loop -- the human decides what to address.\n \n-Validators receive: commit SHA + contract assertions. They do NOT receive proposal, design, or worker reasoning.\n+Validators receive: commit SHA + contract checks. They do NOT receive proposal, design, or worker reasoning.\n \n ### Fix Loop\n - On validation failure: orchestrator writes a symptom-based fix brief (no contract text) to mission/issues/, spawns a fix worker\n@@ -81,7 +81,7 @@ Validators receive: commit SHA + contract assertions. They do NOT receive propos\n \n ### OpenSpec integration\n - `/mission` is the single workflow for all work (small fixes to large epics). Scope adapts naturally.\n-- contract.md is a new OpenSpec artifact (validation assertions, written during planning)\n+- contract.md is a new OpenSpec artifact (validation checks, written during planning)\n - mission/ directory nests inside openspec/changes/<change-name>/\n - Archiving the change archives the mission artifacts too\n \n@@ -137,9 +137,9 @@ For each unchecked task in tasks.md:\n 2. Write brief to mission/briefs/task-N.md\n 3. Kick off worker via Bash `run_in_background` (engine per config.yaml)\n 4. On completion, read mission/handoffs/task-N.md\n-5. Spawn scrutiny validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\n+5. Spawn scrutiny validator (commit SHA + contract checks)\n 6. If fail -> write symptom-based fix brief, spawn fix worker, re-validate both. Circuit breaker: 3 attempts -> ask human.\n-7. Spawn user-test validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\n+7. Spawn user-test validator (commit SHA + contract checks)\n 8. If fail -> same fix/re-validate loop\n 9. If frontend task -> spawn demo video\n 10. Mark task complete in tasks.md, update progress.md\n@@ -216,11 +216,11 @@ openspec/changes/<change-name>/\n # Contract\n \n ## Task 1: [name]\n-- [Assertion verifiable from outside without knowing the code]\n-- [Another assertion]\n+- [Check verifiable from outside without knowing the code]\n+- [Another check]\n \n ## Task 2: [name]\n-- [Assertion]\n+- [Check]\n ```\n \n ## Fix Brief Format\n@@ -229,7 +229,7 @@ openspec/changes/<change-name>/\n # Fix Brief: Task N - [name] (Attempt 2)\n \n ## What Failed\n-[Specific failed assertions from the validator report]\n+[Specific failed checks from the validator report]\n \n ## Validator Output\n [Relevant error messages, test failures, review findings]\ndiff --git a/docs/provenance.md b/docs/provenance.md\nindex a2ed18b..4c19aad 100644\n--- a/docs/provenance.md\n+++ b/docs/provenance.md\n@@ -15,11 +15,11 @@ Source: [docs/reference_factory_talk.md](reference_factory_talk.md)\n | Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory implementation |\n |---------|--------------------|----------------------------|\n | Three-role architecture | Orchestrator, worker, validator as distinct roles | Orchestrator is the /mission session. Workers are dispatched subagents. Validators are separate subagents with isolated prompts. |\n-| Validation contract | \"Defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of assertions\" | contract.md (Phase 5), written after specs/tasks but before the build loop. Workers never see it. |\n+| Validation contract | \"Defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of checks\" | contract.md (Phase 5), written after specs/tasks but before the build loop. Workers never see it. |\n | Adversarial separation | Workers don't see the contract, validators don't see the plan | Stricter than Factory: workers never see contract.md, validators never see proposal.md or design.md. |\n | Serial execution | One worker at a time to avoid conflicts | Rule 1: \"One worker at a time. Never dispatch two workers in parallel.\" |\n | Structured handoffs | Workers produce reports with what was done, commands, exit codes, issues | Handoff template: commit SHA, approach, commands run, concerns. |\n-| Two validator types | Scrutiny validator (tests, lint, review) and user-testing validator (spawns app, interacts) | Scrutiny (Phase 8 Step 4): build/test/lint/review/standards/simplify/security/assertions/behavioral conformance. User-test (Phase 8 Step 6): HTTP tests or browser. |\n+| Two validator types | Scrutiny validator (tests, lint, review) and user-testing validator (spawns app, interacts) | Scrutiny (Phase 8 Step 4): build/test/lint/review/standards/simplify/security/checks/behavioral conformance. User-test (Phase 8 Step 6): HTTP tests or browser. |\n | Multi-model strategy | Different LLMs for different roles | config.yaml: workers default to Codex (GPT-5.4), validators to Claude, adversarial reviewers use one of each. |\n | Read-only parallelization | Only parallelize read-only operations | Only parallel dispatch is the two adversarial reviewers in Phase 9, which are read-only. |\n \n@@ -66,7 +66,7 @@ Concepts in the current implementation that neither source described:\n | Addition | Purpose |\n |----------|---------|\n | Spec coverage audit with deviation classification | Maps every PRD requirement to tasks. Classifies deviations as interpretation, substitution, or removal with escalating approval. |\n-| Contract cross-referencing to PRD | Assertions trace to specific PRD requirements. Deviated items are marked. |\n+| Contract cross-referencing to PRD | Checks trace to specific PRD requirements. Deviated items are marked. |\n | Fix loop with circuit breaker | On validation failure, spawns a fix worker and re-runs both validators. Caps at 3 attempts before escalating to human. |\n | Spec delta check | Post-build verification that every COVERED item in the coverage audit is actually present in the built code. |\n | Final gates | Whole-repo build/test/lint/format after the last task catches regressions from late tasks. |\ndiff --git a/docs/reference_factory_talk.md b/docs/reference_factory_talk.md\nindex 7ed5c99..f7b37d9 100644\n--- a/docs/reference_factory_talk.md\n+++ b/docs/reference_factory_talk.md\n@@ -18,7 +18,7 @@ Source: Talk by Factory's core agent harness lead at AI Engineer Europe (April 2\n **Missions combine 4 of 5** (delegation, creator-verifier, broadcast, negotiation) into a single workflow.\n \n **Three-role architecture:**\n-- **Orchestrators** -- plan features/milestones, ask clarifying questions, produce a \"validation contract\" (defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of assertions)\n+- **Orchestrators** -- plan features/milestones, ask clarifying questions, produce a \"validation contract\" (defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of checks)\n - **Workers** -- implement one feature at a time with clean context, commit to git, structured handoff report (what was done, what's undone, commands/exit codes, issues, procedure compliance)\n - **Validators** -- two types:\n   - *Scrutiny Validator* -- test suite, type checking, lint, dedicated code review agents per feature\ndiff --git a/skills/mission-resume/SKILL.md b/skills/mission-resume/SKILL.md\nindex 555c5e9..08f963f 100644\n--- a/skills/mission-resume/SKILL.md\n+++ b/skills/mission-resume/SKILL.md\n@@ -31,7 +31,7 @@ Read all artifacts for the selected mission:\n | Tasks | `openspec/changes/<change-name>/tasks.md` | Which tasks are done, which remain |\n | Progress log | `mission/progress.md` | Chronological record of what happened |\n | Mission config | `mission/config.yaml` | Engine assignments |\n-| Contract | `contract.md` | Assertions grouped by task for validators |\n+| Contract | `contract.md` | Checks grouped by task for validators |\n | Proposal | `proposal.md` | What is being built and why |\n | Design | `design.md` | Architecture decisions |\n \ndiff --git a/skills/mission/prompts/adversarial-reviewer.md b/skills/mission/prompts/adversarial-reviewer.md\nindex 8368aa8..298ea87 100644\n--- a/skills/mission/prompts/adversarial-reviewer.md\n+++ b/skills/mission/prompts/adversarial-reviewer.md\n@@ -60,11 +60,11 @@ For each commit from the inventory, in order:\n \n - Logic errors or edge cases not covered by tests\n - Cross-file inconsistencies within this commit (types, interfaces, naming)\n-- Contract assertions that technically pass but have subtle flaws\n+- Contract checks that technically pass but have subtle flaws\n - Security issues, race conditions, or data integrity problems\n - Missing error handling or graceful degradation\n - Test doubles leaking into production code paths (NullBridge pattern)\n-- Gaming of contract assertions (smoke test that bypasses real wiring, etc.)\n+- Gaming of contract checks (smoke test that bypasses real wiring, etc.)\n \n For each finding, classify severity as HIGH, MEDIUM, or LOW. Cite file paths and line numbers.\n \n@@ -74,7 +74,7 @@ Once every commit has been reviewed, re-read the `## Task N` sections of your ow\n \n - A type or interface introduced in task N consumed incorrectly by task N+k\n - Race conditions or ordering issues between tasks touching shared state\n-- A contract assertion that no single commit falsifies but the combined behavior does\n+- A contract check that no single commit falsifies but the combined behavior does\n - Integration gaps where task A's outputs do not meet task B's expectations\n \n To verify suspicions, read the **final state** of files from the current working tree (not historical commit blobs). The working tree reflects what will actually ship; commit blobs reflect a point in time that may have been amended by a later fix. Append findings to the `## Cross-task` section.\ndiff --git a/skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md b/skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md\nindex 10e7412..81abf0f 100644\n--- a/skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md\n+++ b/skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md\n@@ -3,8 +3,8 @@ You are a scrutiny validator for the Darkfactory mission system. Your job is to\n ## Commit to Validate\n SHA: <commit-sha>\n \n-## Contract Assertions for This Task\n-<paste the assertions from contract.md for task N>\n+## Contract Checks for This Task\n+<paste the checks from contract.md for task N>\n \n ## Deterministic Pre-passes\n \n@@ -179,19 +179,19 @@ Run these gates in order. For each, report PASS or FAIL with evidence.\n \n 9. **Security review:** If the diff touches auth, secrets, user input, network boundaries, or filesystem trust boundaries (copying/moving/promoting files between directories, extracting archives, sandbox-to-production transfers), run /security. Otherwise \"N/A.\"\n \n-10. **Contract assertions:** Mark each assertion PASS, FAIL, or SKIP with a one-line explanation. SKIPs for work deferred to later tasks: append to `mission/deferrals.md` as `task N -- <assertion>`.\n+10. **Contract checks:** Mark each check PASS, FAIL, or SKIP with a one-line explanation. SKIPs for work deferred to later tasks: append to `mission/deferrals.md` as `task N -- <check>`.\n \n-11. **Behavioral conformance:** For each assertion that describes observable behavior (CLI output, exit codes, file content, API response, shell behavior), write and execute a minimal test script that verifies the behavior by actually running the code. Do not rely solely on reading the code -- run it. Examples:\n-    - CLI flag assertion: run the binary with the flag, check output\n-    - Exit code assertion: run the command, check `$?`\n-    - Shell alias assertion: source the alias, run `type <name>`, check the definition\n-    - API response assertion: curl the endpoint, check the response body\n+11. **Behavioral conformance:** For each check that describes observable behavior (CLI output, exit codes, file content, API response, shell behavior), write and execute a minimal test script that verifies the behavior by actually running the code. Do not rely solely on reading the code -- run it. Examples:\n+    - CLI flag check: run the binary with the flag, check output\n+    - Exit code check: run the command, check `$?`\n+    - Shell alias check: source the alias, run `type <name>`, check the definition\n+    - API response check: curl the endpoint, check the response body\n     \n-    If an assertion cannot be tested via execution (e.g., internal architecture, code organization, build configuration), mark it as \"verified by inspection\" with an explanation of why execution was not possible.\n+    If a check cannot be tested via execution (e.g., internal architecture, code organization, build configuration), mark it as \"verified by inspection\" with an explanation of why execution was not possible.\n     \n     If a behavioral test is inconclusive (e.g., the test script errors out, the environment lacks a dependency), mark it as \"verified by inspection (execution inconclusive: <reason>)\" and fall back to code reading. Do not let an inconclusive execution check block the validation.\n     \n-    Report each assertion's verification method (execution or inspection) in the contract assertions table.\n+    Report each check's verification method (execution or inspection) in the contract checks table.\n \n 12. **Silent skip detection:** Grep test files in the diff for language-specific patterns that cause tests to report PASS while testing nothing. Use the `Detected language` named fact from the deterministic pre-pass to choose the primary language-specific scan, and also inspect file extensions in the diff so mixed-language projects are covered.\n \n@@ -279,23 +279,23 @@ Use this format:\n | Gate 7: Code standards | PASS/FAIL | ... |\n | Gate 8: Code simplify | PASS/FAIL | ... |\n | Gate 9: Security review | PASS/FAIL/N/A | ... |\n-| Gate 10: Contract assertions | PASS/FAIL | ... |\n+| Gate 10: Contract checks | PASS/FAIL | ... |\n | Gate 11: Behavioral conformance | PASS/FAIL/N/A | ... |\n | Gate 12: Silent skip detection | PASS/FAIL/SKIP | ... |\n | Gate 13: Diff coverage | PASS/WARN/FAIL/SKIP | ... |\n | Gate 14: Test quality review | PASS/WARN/FAIL/SKIP | ... |\n \n-## Contract Assertions\n-| # | Assertion | Result | Method | Evidence |\n-|---|-----------|--------|--------|----------|\n-| 1 | <assertion text> | PASS/FAIL | execution/inspection | <one-line evidence> |\n+## Contract Checks\n+| # | Check | Result | Method | Evidence |\n+|---|-------|--------|--------|----------|\n+| 1 | <check text> | PASS/FAIL | execution/inspection | <one-line evidence> |\n | 2 | ... | ... | ... | ... |\n \n-## Failed Assertions (if any)\n+## Failed Checks (if any)\n <detailed explanation of each failure>\n \n ## Recommendations (if FAIL)\n <specific guidance for fixing the failures>\n ```\n \n-IMPORTANT: The overall result is FAIL if ANY gate fails or ANY contract assertion fails. Be strict. A near-miss is still a FAIL.\n+IMPORTANT: The overall result is FAIL if ANY gate fails or ANY contract check fails. Be strict. A near-miss is still a FAIL.\ndiff --git a/skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md b/skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md\nindex 20dd9be..31db48a 100644\n--- a/skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md\n+++ b/skills/mission/prompts/usertest-validator.md\n@@ -3,8 +3,8 @@ You are a user-test validator for the Darkfactory mission system. Your job is to\n ## Commit to Validate\n SHA: <commit-sha>\n \n-## Contract Assertions for This Task\n-<paste the assertions from contract.md for task N>\n+## Contract Checks for This Task\n+<paste the checks from contract.md for task N>\n \n ## Instructions\n \n@@ -14,12 +14,12 @@ SHA: <commit-sha>\n    - **Backend only** (API routes, database, server logic, CLI): Write and run HTTP tests against the API endpoints. Use curl or a test script.\n    - **Frontend only** (UI components, pages, styles): Run /test-app to drive a browser and verify UI behavior.\n    - **Both backend and frontend**: Run HTTP tests first, then /test-app for the UI.\n-   - **Infrastructure only** (config, build, CI): Verify the build passes and config is correct. You may mark behavioral assertions as SKIP with reason \"infrastructure-only change, not testable via HTTP or browser.\"\n+   - **Infrastructure only** (config, build, CI): Verify the build passes and config is correct. You may mark behavioral checks as SKIP with reason \"infrastructure-only change, not testable via HTTP or browser.\"\n \n-3. **Check each contract assertion:** For each assertion, run the appropriate test. Mark PASS, FAIL, or SKIP.\n-   - PASS: The assertion is demonstrably true.\n-   - FAIL: The assertion is demonstrably false, or the test reveals a bug.\n-   - SKIP: The assertion cannot be tested from outside (e.g., internal refactor) or the work is deferred to a later task. Provide a reason. If deferred to a later task, append to `mission/deferrals.md` as `task N -- <assertion>`.\n+3. **Check each contract check:** For each check, run the appropriate test. Mark PASS, FAIL, or SKIP.\n+   - PASS: The check is demonstrably true.\n+   - FAIL: The check is demonstrably false, or the test reveals a bug.\n+   - SKIP: The check cannot be tested from outside (e.g., internal refactor) or the work is deferred to a later task. Provide a reason. If deferred to a later task, append to `mission/deferrals.md` as `task N -- <check>`.\n \n ## Output Format\n \n@@ -41,17 +41,17 @@ Use this format:\n | 1 | <what was tested> | HTTP/Browser/Manual | PASS/FAIL |\n | 2 | ... | ... | ... |\n \n-## Contract Assertions\n-| # | Assertion | Result | Evidence |\n-|---|-----------|--------|----------|\n-| 1 | <assertion text> | PASS/FAIL/SKIP | <evidence or skip reason> |\n+## Contract Checks\n+| # | Check | Result | Evidence |\n+|---|-------|--------|----------|\n+| 1 | <check text> | PASS/FAIL/SKIP | <evidence or skip reason> |\n | 2 | ... | ... | ... |\n \n-## Failed Assertions (if any)\n+## Failed Checks (if any)\n <detailed explanation of each failure>\n \n ## Recommendations (if FAIL)\n <specific guidance for fixing the failures>\n ```\n \n-IMPORTANT: The overall result is FAIL if ANY contract assertion fails (SKIP does not count as failure). Be thorough -- actually run the tests, don't just read the code and guess.\n+IMPORTANT: The overall result is FAIL if ANY contract check fails (SKIP does not count as failure). Be thorough -- actually run the tests, don't just read the code and guess.\ndiff --git a/skills/mission/templates/contract.md b/skills/mission/templates/contract.md\nindex aaa6c55..2e769e9 100644\n--- a/skills/mission/templates/contract.md\n+++ b/skills/mission/templates/contract.md\n@@ -2,10 +2,10 @@\n \n ## Task 1: <name>\n <!-- Specs: capability/1, capability/3 -->\n-- [ ] [execution] <assertion capturing specific spec scenario detail>\n-- [ ] [output] <assertion capturing specific spec scenario detail>\n+- [ ] [execution] <check capturing specific spec scenario detail>\n+- [ ] [output] <check capturing specific spec scenario detail>\n \n ## Task 2: <name>\n <!-- Specs: capability/2, capability/4 (DEVIATED) -->\n-- [ ] [execution] <assertion capturing specific spec scenario detail>\n-- [ ] [inspection] <assertion capturing the deviated behavior as actually implemented>\n+- [ ] [execution] <check capturing specific spec scenario detail>\n+- [ ] [inspection] <check capturing the deviated behavior as actually implemented>\ndiff --git a/skills/mission/templates/plan.md b/skills/mission/templates/plan.md\nindex 347f069..1b7aeb4 100644\n--- a/skills/mission/templates/plan.md\n+++ b/skills/mission/templates/plan.md\n@@ -57,9 +57,9 @@ For each unchecked task in tasks.md (skip `- [~]` tasks):\n 2. Write brief to mission/briefs/task-N.md\n 3. Dispatch worker (engine from config.yaml)\n 4. Read handoff from mission/handoffs/task-N.md\n-5. Spawn scrutiny validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\n+5. Spawn scrutiny validator (commit SHA + contract checks)\n 6. If fail -> write to issues/, spawn fix worker, re-validate. Circuit breaker at 3.\n-7. Spawn user-test validator (commit SHA + contract assertions)\n+7. Spawn user-test validator (commit SHA + contract checks)\n 8. If fail -> same fix/re-validate loop\n 9. If frontend task -> spawn demo video\n 10. Mark task done in tasks.md, update progress.md\ndiff --git a/skills/mission/templates/pr-body.md b/skills/mission/templates/pr-body.md\nindex d3de7cf..1d9888d 100644\n--- a/skills/mission/templates/pr-body.md\n+++ b/skills/mission/templates/pr-body.md\n@@ -19,7 +19,7 @@ Closes #<PRD issue number from plan>\n \n ### Mission Stats\n - Total tasks: <N>\n-- Total assertions: <M>\n+- Total checks: <M>\n - Total fix attempts: <sum>\n - Circuit breakers triggered: <count>\n - Duration: <start to end>\ndiff --git a/skills/mission/templates/worker-fix-brief.md b/skills/mission/templates/worker-fix-brief.md\nindex 5c2e3d5..a593467 100644\n--- a/skills/mission/templates/worker-fix-brief.md\n+++ b/skills/mission/templates/worker-fix-brief.md\n@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@\n # Fix Brief: Task N - <name> (Attempt <attempt+1>)\n \n ## What Failed\n-<describe the observed symptoms -- error messages, unexpected behavior, test failures. Do NOT copy assertion text from the contract. Describe what went wrong, not what the contract expected.>\n+<describe the observed symptoms -- error messages, unexpected behavior, test failures. Do NOT copy check text from the contract. Describe what went wrong, not what the contract expected.>\n \n ## Diagnostic Triage\n <paste the triage subagent's output from mission/issues/task-N-v<attempt>-triage.md. If triage was skipped or failed, write \"Triage unavailable -- diagnose from the validator output below.\">\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
