{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/reference_factory_talk.md | sed -n '1,35p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tname: factory-missions-talk-reference\n     3\tdescription: Reference material from Factory's \"Assembling Agent Teams\" talk at AI Engineer Europe -- core architecture details for the Missions multi-agent system\n     4\ttype: reference\n     5\toriginSessionId: d0be7010-3858-4b92-8153-32045af7ab9b\n     6\t---\n     7\tSource: Talk by Factory's core agent harness lead at AI Engineer Europe (April 2026). Slide images in `/Users/stuart/Downloads/talkimages/` (13 JPEGs). Full transcript and summary provided by Stuart in the initial conversation.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Key Architecture Details\n    10\t\n    11\t**Five multi-agent strategies (taxonomy):**\n    12\t1. Delegation -- parent spawns sub-agents\n    13\t2. Creator-Verifier -- separate agents for building vs checking\n    14\t3. Direct Communication -- agents talk without coordinator (fragile)\n    15\t4. Negotiation -- agents coordinate over shared resources\n    16\t5. Broadcast -- one agent sends info to many\n    17\t\n    18\t**Missions combine 4 of 5** (delegation, creator-verifier, broadcast, negotiation) into a single workflow.\n    19\t\n    20\t**Three-role architecture:**\n    21\t- **Orchestrators** -- plan features/milestones, ask clarifying questions, produce a \"validation contract\" (defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of checks)\n    22\t- **Workers** -- implement one feature at a time with clean context, commit to git, structured handoff report (what was done, what's undone, commands/exit codes, issues, procedure compliance)\n    23\t- **Validators** -- two types:\n    24\t  - *Scrutiny Validator* -- test suite, type checking, lint, dedicated code review agents per feature\n    25\t  - *User Testing Validator* -- QA engine that spawns the app, interacts via computer use, fills forms, clicks buttons, checks flows end-to-end\n    26\t\n    27\t**Execution model:**\n    28\t- Serial feature execution (one worker at a time) to avoid conflicts\n    29\t- Parallelization only for read-only ops (code search, API research) and within validators\n    30\t- Correctness compounds over multi-day runs\n    31\t\n    32\t**Model strategy:**\n    33\t- Model-agnostic: different LLMs for planning (slow reasoning), implementation (fast code fluency), validation (precise instruction following)\n    34\t- Structure compensates for non-frontier models\n    35\t- Almost all orchestration logic is in ~700 lines of prompts/skills, not hard-coded\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQHY4S39BX9YXXDX9GXCFD3Q","ts":"2026-05-01T14:15:26Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/provenance.md | sed -n '1,30p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Provenance\n     2\t\n     3\tHow Darkfactory relates to its two original sources: Factory.ai's multi-agent mission\n     4\tarchitecture and Matt Pocock's AI development skills. Reviewed against the current\n     5\timplementation (SKILL.md) as of 2026-04-12.\n     6\t\n     7\t---\n     8\t\n     9\t## Factory.ai\n    10\t\n    11\tSource: [docs/reference_factory_talk.md](reference_factory_talk.md)\n    12\t\n    13\t### Faithfully implemented\n    14\t\n    15\t| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory implementation |\n    16\t|---------|--------------------|----------------------------|\n    17\t| Three-role architecture | Orchestrator, worker, validator as distinct roles | Orchestrator is the /mission session. Workers are dispatched subagents. Validators are separate subagents with isolated prompts. |\n    18\t| Validation contract | \"Defines done before coding starts, can be hundreds of checks\" | contract.md (Phase 5), written after specs/tasks but before the build loop. Workers never see it. |\n    19\t| Adversarial separation | Workers don't see the contract, validators don't see the plan | Stricter than Factory: workers never see contract.md, validators never see proposal.md or design.md. |\n    20\t| Serial execution | One worker at a time to avoid conflicts | Rule 1: \"One worker at a time. Never dispatch two workers in parallel.\" |\n    21\t| Structured handoffs | Workers produce reports with what was done, commands, exit codes, issues | Handoff template: commit SHA, approach, commands run, concerns. |\n    22\t| Two validator types | Scrutiny validator (tests, lint, review) and user-testing validator (spawns app, interacts) | Scrutiny (Phase 8 Step 4): build/test/lint/review/standards/simplify/security/checks/behavioral conformance. User-test (Phase 8 Step 6): HTTP tests or browser. |\n    23\t| Multi-model strategy | Different LLMs for different roles | config.yaml: workers default to Codex (GPT-5.4), validators to Claude, adversarial reviewers use one of each. |\n    24\t| Read-only parallelization | Only parallelize read-only operations | Only parallel dispatch is the two adversarial reviewers in Phase 9, which are read-only. |\n    25\t\n    26\t### Implemented differently\n    27\t\n    28\t| Concept | Factory description | Darkfactory difference |\n    29\t|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n    30\t| User-test validator | \"Computer use\" QA engine that spawns the app and interacts | Uses HTTP tests and browser automation (/test-app). Same concept, less sophisticated execution. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQHY4S39BX9YXXDX9GXCFD3Q","ts":"2026-05-01T14:15:26Z","tool":"command_execution"}
