ovenance.md:18` puts the text in quotes. If the source actually used \"assertions\", this rename trades source fidelity for project terminology. It is low severity because the task explicitly asked docs to use checks for contract items, but this is the one place where preserving the original wording may matter.\n    32\t\n    33\t## Task 4\n    34\t\n    35\tNo findings. The smoke test found two awkward doubled-check phrasings, and commit `398775c` fixed both in the final source state.\n    36\t\n    37\t## Task 5\n    38\t\n    39\tLOW: `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/handoffs/task-5.md:2` records `cf253937ff71fedcbc67c5c7ae4df3f202d91210`, but the commit that actually ships on this branch is `593c063`. `cf253937...` exists locally but is not contained in any branch, so another clone may not be able to resolve the handoff SHA. This weakens mission traceability.\n    40\t\n    41\t## Task 6\n    42\t\n    43\tHIGH: The module-shape audit depends on glossary changes that are not committed. `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/validations/module-shape-audit.md:6`, `:13`, `:54`, and `:66` say `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` defines `Check`, `Gate`, and `Contract` with the new meanings. The current worktree does contain those edits, but `git diff --name-only` shows `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` is still modified and unstaged, while `git show HEAD:UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` still contains the old committed terms:\n    44\t\n    45\t- line 16: `Contract` is \"A set of assertions...\"\n    46\t- line 17: `Assertion` is the contract item term\n    47\t- line 30: `Behavioral conformance` is still \"check (check 11)\"\n    48\t- line 106: `A Contract contains Assertions grouped by Task`\n    49\t- line 180: validation is resolved as checking `Assertions`\n    50\t- line 182: `Check` is still defined as a scrutiny validator step\n    51\t\n    52\tThis means the branch as committed contradicts the new terminology and contradicts its own task-6/task-8 validation artifacts. The fix is to commit the `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` update, or remove the audit/doc claims that the glossary was updated.\n    53\t\n    54\tLOW: `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/handoffs/task-6.md:2` records `cc9484f`, but the branch contains the rebased commit as `06c1e27`. Like task 5, this can break handoff SHA resolution outside this local repo.\n    55\t\n    56\t## Task 7\n    57\t\n    58\tNo source findings. The task appears to be a no-op because `CLAUDE.md` and `ARCH.md` already existed and were intentionally left unchanged.\n    59\t\n    60\t## Task 8\n    61\t\n    62\tCovered by the Task 6 finding. `openspec/changes/user-friendly-contract-language/mission/handoffs/task-8.md:3` says `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` correctly reserves assertion for code tests, but that is true only in the uncommitted worktree, not in `HEAD`.\n    63\t\n    64\t## Cross-task\n    65\t\n    66\tThe living source files under `skills/mission/`, `skills/mission-resume/`, `README.md`, and `docs/` satisfy the requested rename in the committed branch. The main cross-task defect is that the glossary was used as validation evidence without being included in the committed diff. This creates a split-brain final state: prompts and docs say checks/gates, while the committed ubiquitous language still says assertions/checks with the old meanings.\n    67\t\n    68\tRecommended fix:\n    69\t\n    70\t1. Commit `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md`.\n    71\t2. Update task-5 and task-6 handoff SHAs to the branch commits (`593c063` and `06c1e27`) if these handoffs are meant to be durable artifacts.\n    72\t3. Decide whether source/provenance quote text should preserve original source wording or use Darkfactory's current terms.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
